# BW-80 (the Sinclair ZX80), Machine 140: docs/machines/140-bw80/BW80.md, BW80_DESIGN.md.
# Grant Searle's redrawn issue 2 schematic (searle.x10host.com/zx80/zx80.html, his corrections
# applied: R7 and R28 on D6'), every chip at its designator, running the ZX80's own ROM
# (roms/zx80_ic2.bin). P5v built the display (docs/p5v-bw80-video/BW80_VIDEO_DESIGN.md §5), P5x
# added the keyboard (J3: the membrane keyboard in its own window, the isolating diodes D3-D10
# inside it) and the expansion bus edge connector (J4, nothing plugged in). Left out: the
# cassette (MIC, and the EAR network: EAR is pulled down through R1), the modulator and the 7805;
# D11 is not fitted (the 50 Hz machine).
#
# The Z80A runs at 3.25 MHz, 6.5 MHz halved by IC18 and buffered by two paralleled 74LS05s (IC15).
# The ROM (IC2) answers with A14 and A12 low, the 1K of RAM (IC3, IC4) with A14 high, mirrored
# up to $FFFF. The CPU's data bus D0-D7 meets the memories' MD0-MD7 (the drawing's D0'-D7')
# through 1K resistors R4-R11, so the eight open-collector NOP gates (IC14, IC15) can pull D low
# while the RAM drives MD: a fetch from the display file's echo (A15 high) of a code with bit 6
# clear runs as a NOP, and the data latch IC5 keeps the code. In the refresh, the multiplexers
# IC6-IC8 give the ROM the line counter (IC21) and the code in place of A0-A8, I = $0E on A9-A11:
# the glyph row comes out of the character set at $0E00. At the next rise of the CPU clock,
# C11/R25 make a short pulse that loads the glyph into IC9 from the CPU side of the bus, and
# C9/R2 shift it at each rise of 6.5 MHz. Bit 7 of the code inverts the dots through the IC12
# latch and IC20. The HALT that ends a row is not forced; when R takes A6 low in a refresh, the
# interrupt's acknowledge presets IC18's latch and IC19 counts two M1s to start and two to end
# HSYNC (SYNC low). An IN from an even port (KBD_N) resets the IC11 latch: SYNC held low (VSYNC)
# and the line counter cleared; any OUT sets it again. The keyboard's rows are A8-A15: the ROM
# reads an even port with one of them low, and IC10 puts the five columns KB0-KB4 (pulled up by
# R13-R17, a held key pulling its column low through the keyboard's diode) on D0-D4.
#
# Stand-ins: X1 for the ceramic resonator and IC20's two oscillator gates (inputs tied); RST1 for
# the reset's R21 and C10 (1 ms in place of about 0.2 s). The VIDEO connector takes SYNC and
# VIDEO apart where the ZX80's modulator mixes them.

part J1   POWER
part J2   VIDEO
part J3   MEMBRANEKB        # the keyboard (a window): rows A8-A15, columns KB0-KB4
part J4   EXPANSION         # the expansion bus edge connector, nothing plugged in
part X1   OSC freq=6.5MHz
part RST1 POR time=1ms      # the ZX80's R21/C10
part IC1  Z80
part IC2  2332 select=LL contents=../roms/zx80_ic2.bin
part IC3  2114 access=300ns
part IC4  2114 access=300ns # a 2114-1 on the parts list
part IC5  74LS373           # RAM data latch: the character code
part IC6  74LS157           # ROM address A8, ROM select, A14', A15'
part IC7  74LS157           # ROM address A4-A7
part IC8  74LS157           # ROM address A0-A3
part IC9  74LS165           # dots out
part IC10 74LS365           # keyboard and cassette onto D
part IC11 74LS00            # inverse strobe and set, VSYNC latch
part IC12 74LS00            # inverse latch, RAM and ROM select
part IC13 74LS04
part IC14 74LS05            # NOP gates
part IC15 74LS05            # NOP enable, two NOP gates, M1 clock, CPU clock
part IC16 74LS10            # latch enable, NOP condition, load
part IC17 74LS32            # I/O decoding, NOP
part IC18 74LS74            # sync latch, 6.5 MHz / 2
part IC19 74LS74            # HSYNC
part IC20 74LS86            # 2-phi buffer, inverse video
part IC21 74LS93            # line counter
part R1   R value=1k        # EAR pull-down
part R2   R value=470       # shift clock pull-down (C9)
part R3   R value=1k        # NOP_EN pull-up
part R4   R value=1k        # data bus
part R5   R value=1k
part R6   R value=1k
part R7   R value=1k
part R8   R value=1k
part R9   R value=1k
part R10  R value=1k
part R11  R value=1k
part R12  R value=1k        # NMI_N pull-up
part R13  R value=47k       # keyboard pull-ups
part R14  R value=47k
part R15  R value=47k
part R16  R value=47k
part R17  R value=47k
part R18  R value=1k        # M1 clock pull-up
part R19  R value=2k2       # RAM select, isolated
part R20  R value=330       # CPU clock pull-up
part R21  R value=220k      # RESET_N pull-up
part R22  R value=1k        # BUSRQ_N pull-up
part R23  R value=1k        # WAIT_N pull-up
part R24  R value=1k        # load to the inverse set gate
part R25  R value=470       # load pulse pull-down (C11)
part R28  R value=47k       # MD6 pull-up: D6 reads high on a keyboard read (UK, no D11)
part C9   C value=47p       # 2-phi edges: the shift clock
part C11  C value=47p       # phi rises: the load pulse

# Clocks: 6.5 MHz (DOT2, 2-phi), the CPU's 3.25 MHz (PHI, and CLK through IC15).
net DOT       X1.OUT IC20.1B
net DOT2      IC20.1Y IC18.2CLK C9.1
net PHI       IC18.2Q C11.1
net PHI_N     IC18.2Q_N IC18.2D IC15.5A IC15.6A IC16.1B J4.PHI
net CLK       IC15.5Y IC15.6Y R20.2 IC1.CLK
net VCC       R20.1 IC18.2PRE_N IC18.2CLR_N
net GND       IC20.1A IC20.3A IC20.3B IC20.4A IC20.4B

# CPU: the reset; NMI, bus request and wait pulled up; INT_N on A6.
net RESET_N   IC1.RESET_N RST1.RST_N R21.2 J4.RESET_N
net NMI_N     IC1.NMI_N R12.2 J4.NMI_N
net BUSRQ_N   IC1.BUSRQ_N R22.2 J4.BUSRQ_N
net WAIT_N    IC1.WAIT_N R23.2 J4.WAIT_N
net VCC       R21.1 R12.1 R22.1 R23.1 J4.VCC5
net GND       J4.GND1 J4.GND2

# Address bus.
net A0        IC1.A0 IC3.A0 IC4.A0 IC8.3B IC17.4B J4.A0
net A1        IC1.A1 IC3.A1 IC4.A1 IC8.2B J4.A1
net A2        IC1.A2 IC3.A2 IC4.A2 IC8.4B J4.A2
net A3        IC1.A3 IC3.A3 IC4.A3 IC8.1B J4.A3
net A4        IC1.A4 IC3.A4 IC4.A4 IC7.3B J4.A4
net A5        IC1.A5 IC3.A5 IC4.A5 IC7.2B J4.A5
net A6        IC1.A6 IC3.A6 IC4.A6 IC7.1B IC1.INT_N J4.A6 J4.INT_N
net A7        IC1.A7 IC3.A7 IC4.A7 IC7.4B J4.A7
net A8        IC1.A8 IC3.A8 IC4.A8 IC6.1B J4.A8 J3.A8
net A9        IC1.A9 IC3.A9 IC4.A9 IC2.A9 J4.A9 J3.A9
net A10       IC1.A10 IC2.A10 J4.A10 J3.A10
net A11       IC1.A11 IC2.A11 J4.A11 J3.A11
net A12       IC1.A12 IC2.CS2 J4.A12 J3.A12
net A13       IC1.A13 J4.A13 J3.A13
net A14       IC1.A14 IC6.3A IC6.3B J4.A14 J3.A14
net A15       IC1.A15 IC6.4A IC6.4B J4.A15 J3.A15

# Control lines.
net M1_N      IC1.M1_N IC17.3B IC15.4A J4.M1_N
net MREQ_N    IC1.MREQ_N IC13.1A IC16.3C IC11.1A J4.MREQ_N
net MREQ      IC13.1Y IC12.3A IC12.4A IC16.1C
net IORQ_N    IC1.IORQ_N IC17.1B IC17.2B IC18.1PRE_N J4.IORQ_N
net RD_N      IC1.RD_N IC17.1A J4.RD_N
net WR_N      IC1.WR_N IC3.WE_N IC4.WE_N IC17.2A J4.WR_N
net RFSH_N    IC1.RFSH_N IC16.1A IC6.S IC7.S IC8.S J4.RFSH_N
net HALT_N    IC1.HALT_N IC16.2A J4.HALT_N
net BUSAK_N   IC1.BUSAK_N J4.BUSAK_N

# CPU data bus: the NOP gates, the keyboard buffer, the shift register, the resistors.
net D0        IC1.D0 IC14.6Y IC10.Y3 IC9.A R10.2
net D1        IC1.D1 IC15.3Y IC10.Y4 IC9.B R11.2
net D2        IC1.D2 IC14.2Y IC10.Y5 IC9.C R8.2
net D3        IC1.D3 IC14.3Y IC10.Y2 IC9.D R5.2
net D4        IC1.D4 IC14.4Y IC10.Y6 IC9.E R4.2
net D5        IC1.D5 IC14.5Y IC9.F R6.2
net D6        IC1.D6 IC15.2Y IC9.G R7.2
net D7        IC1.D7 IC14.1Y IC10.Y1 IC9.H R9.2

# Memory data bus (D0'-D7'): the ROM, the RAM, the data latch.
net MD0       IC2.O0 IC3.IO3 IC5.3D R10.1 J4.D0
net MD1       IC2.O1 IC3.IO2 IC5.6D R11.1 J4.D1
net MD2       IC2.O2 IC3.IO4 IC5.7D R8.1 J4.D2
net MD3       IC2.O3 IC4.IO2 IC5.8D R5.1 J4.D3
net MD4       IC2.O4 IC4.IO1 IC5.1D R4.1 J4.D4
net MD5       IC2.O5 IC4.IO3 IC5.2D R6.1 J4.D5
net MD6       IC2.O6 IC4.IO4 IC5.4D R7.1 R28.2 IC13.3A J4.D6
net MD7       IC2.O7 IC3.IO1 IC5.5D R9.1 J4.D7
net VCC       R28.1

# ROM address: the CPU's A0-A8 (B), or in the refresh the line counter and the code (A).
net GND       IC6.G_N IC7.G_N IC8.G_N IC6.2A
net RA0       IC8.3Y IC2.A0
net RA1       IC8.2Y IC2.A1
net RA2       IC8.4Y IC2.A2
net RA3       IC8.1Y IC2.A3
net RA4       IC7.3Y IC2.A4
net RA5       IC7.2Y IC2.A5
net RA6       IC7.1Y IC2.A6
net RA7       IC7.4Y IC2.A7
net RA8       IC6.1Y IC2.A8
net A14B      IC6.3Y IC12.3B IC13.6A
net A15B      IC6.4Y IC16.2B
net ROM_CS1   IC6.2Y IC2.CS1

# Memory selects: RAM with A14 high, ROM with A14 low (and A12 low, on CS2); IC6 keeps the ROM
# selected through the refresh.
net A14B_N    IC13.6Y IC12.4B
net RAM_SEL_N IC12.3Y R19.1
net RAM_CS_N  R19.2 IC3.CS_N IC4.CS_N J4.RAMCS_N
net ROM_SEL_N IC12.4Y IC6.2B

# Data latch: transparent while MREQ is low with RFSH_N high and phi low; frozen in the refresh.
net LE_N      IC16.1Y IC13.2A
net LE        IC13.2Y IC5.LE
net GND       IC5.OE_N
net CH0       IC5.3Q IC8.1A
net CH1       IC5.6Q IC7.3A
net CH2       IC5.7Q IC7.2A
net CH3       IC5.8Q IC7.1A
net CH4       IC5.1Q IC7.4A
net CH5       IC5.2Q IC6.1A
net CH6       IC5.4Q IC13.5A
net CH7       IC5.5Q IC11.2A
net CH6_N     IC13.5Y IC16.3B

# NOP: an M1 fetch in the echo (A15) of a code with bit 6 clear, not halted.
net MD6_N     IC13.3Y IC16.2C
net NOP_COND_N IC16.2Y IC17.3A
net NOP_N     IC17.3Y IC15.1A
net NOP_EN    IC15.1Y R3.2 IC14.1A IC14.2A IC14.3A IC14.4A IC14.5A IC14.6A IC15.2A IC15.3A
net VCC       R3.1

# Dots: the load pulse at each rise of phi (C11, R25) loads IC9 when the latched code is a
# character and MREQ_N is high; C9 and R2 clock it at each rise of 2-phi; QH_N (link A-C:
# black on white) through IC20 with the inverse latch to VIDEO.
net SHIFT_CLK IC9.CLK C9.2 R2.1
net LOAD_PULSE IC16.3A IC11.1B C11.2 R25.1
net GND       R2.2 R25.2 IC9.SER IC9.CLK_INH
net LOAD_N    IC16.3Y IC9.SH_LD_N IC13.4A
net LOAD      IC13.4Y R24.1
net LOAD_R    R24.2 IC11.2B
net PIX_N     IC9.QH_N IC20.2A

# Inverse latch: reset at each load pulse with MREQ_N high, set by the load of a code with bit 7.
net INV_STROBE_N IC11.1Y IC12.1B
net INV_SET_N IC11.2Y IC12.2B
net INV_N     IC12.1Y IC12.2A
net INV       IC12.2Y IC12.1A IC20.2B
net VIDEO     IC20.2Y J2.VIDEO

# I/O: the keyboard at every even read (KBD_N); EAR (no cassette) on D7.
net IORD_N    IC17.1Y IC17.4A
net IOWR_N    IC17.2Y IC11.3A
net KBD_N     IC17.4Y IC10.G1_N IC10.G2_N IC11.4B
net EAR       IC10.A1 R1.1
net KB0       IC10.A3 R13.1 J3.KB0
net KB1       IC10.A4 R14.1 J3.KB1
net KB2       IC10.A5 R15.1 J3.KB2
net KB3       IC10.A2 R16.1 J3.KB3
net KB4       IC10.A6 R17.1 J3.KB4
net VCC       R13.2 R14.2 R15.2 R16.2 R17.2
net GND       R1.2

# VSYNC latch: a keyboard read resets it (VS_N low: SYNC held low, the line counter cleared),
# any OUT sets it.
net VS_N      IC11.3Y IC11.4A IC19.1CLR_N
net VS_RESET  IC11.4Y IC11.3B IC21.R01 IC21.R02

# HSYNC: any IORQ presets IC18's latch; two rises of M1 later IC19 takes SYNC low and clears the
# latch, two more and SYNC is high again (20 T-states with the ROM's interrupt routine).
net M1_CLK    IC15.4Y R18.2 IC19.2CLK IC19.1CLK
net VCC       R18.1 IC18.1CLK IC19.2PRE_N IC19.2CLR_N IC19.1PRE_N
net GND       IC18.1D
net SYNC_ARM_N IC18.1Q_N IC19.2D
net SYNC_NEXT IC19.2Q IC19.1D
net SYNC      IC19.1Q IC18.1CLR_N IC21.CKB J2.SYNC_N

# Line counter: counts HSYNCs (each fall of SYNC), cleared through VSYNC.
net LC0       IC21.QB IC8.3A
net LC1       IC21.QC IC8.2A
net LC2       IC21.QD IC8.4A
net GND       IC21.CKA
